defmodule CommonplaceNext.TrustRootInventoryTest do
  @moduledoc """
  ⭐ THE CONSUMING DOOR FOR THE TRUST-ROOT GATE.

  `bin/check-trust-root-inventory.sh` refuses a Realm holding any trust root tha
t is not its own
  signing key. It exists because the standing "no second trust root" rule was a
RULING AND NOT A
  TECHNICAL OBSTACLE: `Realm.Keystore.child_spec/1` merges
  `Application.get_env(:commonplace_next, Realm.Keystore, [])`, so `roots:` or `
path:` installs one
  with NO SOURCE DIFF AT ALL. A remembered rule does not fire; a filed artifact
does.

  ⛔ WHY NOT A `gate` LINE IN `bin/land-round.sh` -- measured, not assumed: its
GATE_COVERAGE block
  refuses any landing whose pre-merge bytes differ from the blob being pushed. A
 round adding a gate
  line there cannot land, correctly, because such a run would not have executed
the gate it ships.
  So the door is `gate "mix test"`, which is already wired.

  ⚠️ ⚠️  THE GATE'S EXIT CONDITION, NAMED RATHER THAN LEFT IMPLICIT. This gate mea
sures THE
  ENVIRONMENT IT IS RUN IN, and prints which one. Under the landing that is the
ambient dev
  configuration. It therefore does NOT certify a production deployment: `config/
runtime.exs` and
  `config/prod.exs` are read only when a Realm boots under `MIX_ENV=prod`. ⇒ A d
eployment must run
  this gate ON THE DEPLOYMENT. Until something is served that is a limit and not
 a hole -- and it
  goes stale the moment anything is served.
  """
  use ExUnit.Case, async: false

  alias Mix.Tasks.CommonplaceNext.CheckTrustRoots

  @falsifier "bin/test-trust-root-inventory.sh"

  test "this Realm holds exactly its own signing key as a trust root" do
    # ⭐ Called in-process rather than through a subprocess: the same shipped fu
nction the gate runs,
    # against the Realm this suite actually booted. No copy, no second boot.
    case CheckTrustRoots.measure() do
      {:ok, lines} ->
        output = Enum.join(lines, "\n")

        # rc-equivalent alone is satisfied by a measurement that measured nothin
g. These assert the
        # instrument saw a Realm: a non-empty root list, and every root reconstr
ucting to the signer.
        assert output =~ ~r/TRUST ROOTS IN A BOOTED REALM: (\d+) declared=\1\b/
        assert output =~ ~r/ACTIVE TRUST ROOTS IN A BOOTED REALM: (\d+) declared
=\1\b/
        assert output =~ "proves=realm-signer"
        refute output =~ "proves=FOREIGN"
        assert output =~ "VERDICT: TRUST ROOT INVENTORY OK"

      {:red, lines, problems} ->
        flunk("""
        the trust root inventory is RED -- this Realm holds a root it did not ge
nerate,
        or more roots than declared.

        #{Enum.join(lines, "\n")}

        #{Enum.join(problems, "\n")}
        """)

      {:blind, message} ->
        flunk("""
        the trust root gate is BLIND, which is not a passing state: it reported
no number at all.

        #{message}
        """)
    end
  end

